  "prompt": "You generate Playwright code to interact with websites.\n - If you want to click a generic button (e.g. that belongs to an element), use the full `.get_by_role()` path to\n   the element (e.g. .get_by_role(\"group\", name=\"Test Item\").get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\")\n   instead of .get_by_role(\"button\", name=\"Go\"), as this is ambiguous)\n - Write exceptionally *correct* Python code.\n - You love to take advantage of functions in the knowledge_base whenever possible. You use them via Python function calls.\n   It is required to use the knowledge base function corresponding to an action if it exists.\n - Use relative `goto` when you can.\n\nYour task is Use the site search bar to search for the keyword 'crypto', and click on the third-to-last result..\n\nYou are currently on a webpage titled 'Epoch & Drift Benchmark', with the url 'http://localhost:8000/'.\n\nConsider the following Accessibility Tree.\nThe accessibility tree is formatted like this:\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties] {{\n    [child1]\n}};\n[role] [\"name\"] [properties]; // no children\n\nHere is an example:\n<example>\n\narticle \"Willy Wonka's Chocolate Factory Opens Its Gates\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle \"Hogwarts Begins Accepting Applicants for Class of 2029\" {{\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n... to select the first link, you can do (because name = case-insensitive substring match unless exact=True is supplied):\n\n```python3\nfirst_link = page.get_by_role(\"article\", name=\"Willy Wonka\").get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n</example>\n\nHere is another example:\n<example>\narticle {{\n    header \"How can I install CUDA on Ubuntu 22?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\narticle {{\n    header \"How do I install Docker?\";\n    link \"Share\";\n}};\n\n\n... to select the first link, you can do:\n\n```python3\npage.get_by_role(\"article\").filter(has=page.get_by_role(\"header\", \"How can I install CUDA\")).get_by_role(\"link\", name=\"Share\")\n```\n\nHere, the .filter() is necessary to ensure that we select the correct `article`. This is especially important in cases where the parent\nelement doesn't include relevant criteria for identifying a child element.\n</example>\n\n\nTips about this tree:\n - If you see a node as a child of an `iframe`, you must use `page.frame(name=...)`, and *then* access the node (via `.get_by_role` or similar).\n - Some elements will require interaction via `.select_option()`. They will be labeled as such.\n   This is because they are HTML `<select>` elements, and thus cannot be interacted with via clicks.\n\nYou also have this library of Python functions available to you. Think carefully about whether you think these can be used in your code. If you conclude that you can use a function, then simply call it. These functions are all available as global variables.\n<functions>\n# Skill: async def create_curated_collection_by_search(\n    page,\n    keyword: str,\n    max_results: int = 0,\n    output_csv: str = \"\",\n    resolve_links: bool = True,\n)\nUse the site's visible search widget (expected to live in an aside/complementary\nregion) to create a curated topic collection page for `keyword`, then scrape the\nresulting search-results page and return discovered posts.\n\nImportant initial UI state (PRECONDITIONS) -- be explicit:\n- THIS FUNCTION'S FIRST ACTION IS `await page.goto('/')`. The caller should not\n  rely on any other page state before calling; the function navigates to the\n  site root and expects the search UI to be available there.\n- After navigation to '/', the page MUST expose a region with role=\"complementary\"\n  that contains the site's search input. The search input is commonly role=\"searchbox\"\n  (often labelled \"Search\"); if not present the function falls back to a role=\"textbox\"\n  inside the same complementary region.\n- The complementary region should include a control that triggers the search. The\n  preferred control is a role=\"button\" whose visible name includes the word\n  \"search\" (case-insensitive). If that is absent the function falls back to\n  clicking the first role=\"button\" inside the complementary region. If there is\n  no button the function will press Enter in the located search input as a last\n  accessibility-role-centric fallback.\n- The search action must place results inside a region with role=\"main\" that\n  contains role=\"article\" children. If your site renders results elsewhere,\n  modify the scraping selectors in your caller or copy this function and adapt it.\n- Do NOT call this helper while a modal, overlay or cookie banner blocks the\n  complementary or main regions; this function does NOT dismiss overlays.\n\nBehavior & implementation notes (mechanics and observed quirks):\n- The function begins with `await page.goto('/')` to create a reproducible starting\n  state. If you want to avoid that navigation, copy the function and remove/modify\n  the initial goto before calling.\n- Uses accessibility-tree-centric selectors exclusively (page.get_by_role and .nth()).\n- Every locator that is indexed with `.nth()` is preceded by `await locator.count()`\n  to avoid indexing errors when roles are absent.\n- Small, local try/except blocks are used only around fragile DOM reads\n  (inner_text() and get_attribute()) and around the locator-based wait for the\n  results container. There is NO broad/global try/except that hides structural\n  or page-state errors.\n- After triggering the search the function prefers to wait for the results\n  container via `await page.get_by_role('main').wait_for(...)`. If that times out\n  it falls back to `await page.wait_for_load_state('networkidle')`. If your site\n  uses client-side updates without changing load state, replace the fallback\n  waiting strategy in your caller with a more specific locator-based wait.\n- Href resolution: when `resolve_links` is True, discovered hrefs are resolved\n  against the final page.url using urllib.parse.urljoin so relative links become\n  absolute. If you prefer raw hrefs, set `resolve_links=False`.\n\nParameters:\n- page: Playwright Page object (first argument).\n- keyword: search term to query the site's search widget.\n- max_results: maximum number of articles to return. 0 means no limit.\n- output_csv: optional path to write a UTF-8 CSV with header [title, date, author, url].\n  Empty string disables CSV output.\n- resolve_links: whether to resolve discovered hrefs to absolute URLs.\n\nReturns:\n- List[dict]: each dict contains keys 'title' and 'url' and may include 'date' and\n  'author' (empty string when not found).\n\nUsage log (runs I've performed):\n- Development run (local dev server / action history):\n  * Call: await create_curated_collection_by_search(page, 'Excel')\n  * Observed behavior: function navigated to '/'; located role=\"complementary\"\n    containing a role=\"searchbox\" named \"Search\" and a visible Search button.\n    It filled the input with \"Excel\" and activated the search (clicked the Search\n    button). The function waited for role=\"main\" to appear and then scraped\n    role=\"article\" entries. Returned one matching article:\n      [\n        {\n          'title': 'You need to know what the tilde (~) does in Excel',\n          'url': 'http://localhost:8000/?p=6',\n          'date': 'December 26, 2025',\n          'author': 'admin'\n        }\n      ]\n\nObserved quirks & recommendations:\n- Many themes use an icon-only search button. If the visible-text-based lookup\n  fails, clicking the first button inside the complementary region is a pragmatic\n  fallback but may click an unrelated control. If that happens, refine the selector\n  (for example by using an aria-label) before calling.\n- If search results render client-side (no full navigation), waiting for\n  `networkidle` may not be appropriate; replace the fallback wait with a locator\n  wait in your environment.\n- inner_text() often contains decorative glyphs or extra whitespace; the function\n  strips returned strings but callers may want to sanitize further.\n\n# Skill: async def create_curated_collection_and_newsletter(\n    page,\n    keyword: str,\n    max_search_results: int = 0,\n    curated_csv: str = \"\",\n    newsletter_path: str = \"\",\n    max_articles: int = 10,\n    max_excerpt_len: int = 800,\n    resolve_links: bool = True,\n)\nPerform a site search to create a curated collection (CSV) and also compile a\nweekly newsletter (Markdown) from the site's recent posts.\n\nHigh-level behavior\n- FIRST ACTION: always calls `await page.goto('/')` to establish a reproducible\n  starting state.\n- Uses the existing site-aware helper `create_curated_collection_by_search(...)`\n  to perform the visible search and optionally write a CSV of results.\n- Uses `create_weekly_newsletter_draft(...)` to produce a Markdown digest from\n  the site's recent posts and optionally save it to a file.\n- Returns a dict with keys \"curated_posts\" (the list produced by the search\n  helper) and \"newsletter\" (the Markdown string produced by the newsletter\n  helper).\n\nNotes & important preconditions\n- The function expects the root page ('/') to expose a complementary region\n  containing a search input (role=\"searchbox\" or role=\"textbox\") and a\n  trigger control (a button, or pressing Enter). If a modal or cookie banner\n  blocks the complementary/main regions, dismiss those first.\n- Both delegated helpers call `page.goto('/')` themselves, so a single call\n  to this function may navigate to '/' multiple times. This is intentional\n  for reproducibility but can be slightly inefficient.\n\nParameters\n- page: Playwright Page object (first argument).\n- keyword: the search term to query the site's search widget.\n- max_search_results: maximum number of search-result posts to return; 0 means no limit.\n- curated_csv: optional path to write the CSV of curated results. If empty,\n  a default filename will be created (curated_<safe_keyword>.csv).\n- newsletter_path: optional path to write the newsletter Markdown. If empty,\n  a default filename will be created (newsletter_<safe_keyword>.md).\n- max_articles: maximum number of articles to include in the compiled newsletter.\n- max_excerpt_len: maximum characters to include per post excerpt in the newsletter.\n- resolve_links: whether to resolve discovered links to absolute URLs.\n\nReturn value\n- A dict: {\"curated_posts\": <list-of-dicts>, \"newsletter\": <markdown-string>}\n\nObserved quirks & recommendations (from runs)\n- Usage log (run performed):\n  * Call used in action history:\n      await create_curated_collection_and_newsletter(page, \"Excel\", max_search_results=0, curated_csv=\"curated_excel.csv\", newsletter_path=\"newsletter_from_search.md\")\n    Observed behavior: the call wrote 10 results to curated_excel.csv and\n    returned a list of 10 post dicts (including the Excel post). The weekly\n    newsletter MD was produced and saved to newsletter_from_search.md; the\n    newsletter contained multiple recent posts including the Excel post.\n- The search button on some themes is icon-only or a `javascript:` pseudo-URL.\n  The delegated helper uses pragmatic fallbacks (first button in complementary\n  region or pressing Enter). If search activation fails in your environment,\n  adapt/create a more specific selector (for example an aria-label) and call\n  the underlying helper directly.\n- inner_text() on the site commonly includes decorative glyphs and extra\n  whitespace; both helpers trim and collapse whitespace but downstream\n  sanitization may still be useful.\n- Because both helpers navigate to '/', expect multiple navigations during\n  one call. If you need to optimize for speed, call the helpers from a page\n  already at the root and avoid the initial goto in this wrapper.\n\nImplementation notes\n- This wrapper delegates to the existing helpers rather than reimplementing\n  the scraping logic. That keeps the function concise and leverages already\n  tested behavior.\n\n\n</functions>\n\nPlease think step by step and determine if further action needs to be taken. \nUse Playwright and complete the following Python function declaration:\n\nasync def act(page):\n    ...\n\nIf further action needs to be taken, provide a code snippet to interact with the page. \nPlease be noted that if you call an API that returns information retrieved from the website, you must return the result as-is, without any processing, filtering, or transformation. Do not attempt to interpret the data, extract values, or apply conditions. \nPlease return the API response results or print it out exactly as received. \n\n\nIf you have no more actions to perform, leave the `python_code` field blank and set the `terminate_with_result` field of your response to a non-empty string. Make sure that your terminate_with_result value is a direct response to the original task query.\n\nYour previous actions are:\n<previous_actions>\n<reasoning>\n1) I will use the knowledge-base helper create_curated_collection_by_search because it is the site-aware helper designed to operate the visible search widget from the complementary region. The helper's precondition and behavior (it begins with await page.goto('/')) match the expected approach for performing a reproducible search.\n\n2) After calling the helper with keyword 'crypto', the page should show search results inside a region with role=\"main\" that contains role=\"article\" children. I will locate those articles via accessibility-role-centric selectors (page.get_by_role(\"main\").get_by_role(\"article\")).\n\n3) To click the third-to-last result I compute the index as count - 3. Because any .nth() indexing requires first checking the locator.count(), I call await articles.count() before using .nth().\n\n4) Within the target article I aim to click the first role=\"link\" child (if present). If no link exists I fall back to clicking the article itself.\n\n5) The final action is the click that navigates away from the search results; per instructions this click is left as the last action in the function.\n\nUtility of knowledge-base functions:\n- create_curated_collection_by_search: Highly relevant and required for the search action because it encapsulates the search activation, waits, and scraping behavior in an accessibility-role-centric way. I used it to perform the search for 'crypto'.\n- create_curated_collection_and_newsletter: Not used because its behavior is higher-level (also produces a newsletter) and still calls the search helper; the task only requires performing the search and clicking the third-to-last result.\n\nNo further actions are required in this step beyond clicking the third-to-last result; the click is performed as the last operation and may navigate away from the page.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    \"\"\"\n    Plan:\n    - Use the site-aware helper create_curated_collection_by_search to perform the visible search for 'crypto'.\n      This helper navigates to '/' and triggers the site's search UI according to the accessibility-tree-centric rules.\n    - After the helper returns (and the page shows search results in role=\"main\" with role=\"article\" children), locate the list\n      of articles via accessibility roles, compute the third-to-last index, and click the first link inside that article.\n\n    Notes on knowledge-base functions:\n    - create_curated_collection_by_search is the exact helper that performs a visible search using the complementary/search UI\n      and collects results. It is therefore appropriate and required to use it for the search step.\n    - After calling it, we rely on the search results rendered in role=\"main\" and interact with the accessibility-role-based locators\n      to click the desired article. The final click is expected to navigate away, so it is the last action.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use the KB helper to perform the visible search. This helper will navigate to '/' and use the complementary search UI.\n    posts = await create_curated_collection_by_search(page, \"crypto\", max_results=0, resolve_links=True)\n\n    # After the helper runs, search results should be rendered in role=\"main\" containing role=\"article\" children.\n    main = page.get_by_role(\"main\")\n    articles = main.get_by_role(\"article\")\n\n    # Guard: get count before indexing\n    count = await articles.count()\n    if count < 3:\n        # Not enough results to click the third-to-last item; nothing to do.\n        return\n\n    target_index = count - 3\n    target_article = articles.nth(target_index)\n\n    # Prefer to click the first accessible link inside the article. Count links first to avoid indexing errors.\n    link_locator = target_article.get_by_role(\"link\")\n    link_count = await link_locator.count()\n    if link_count > 0:\n        await link_locator.nth(0).click()\n    else:\n        # Fallback: click the article itself if no link is present.\n        await target_article.click()\n\n</code>\n\n<stdout>\ncreate_curated_collection_by_search: complementary region not found on '/'. Aborting.\n\n</stdout>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\n1) I considered using the knowledge-base helper create_curated_collection_by_search because it is the site-aware helper that activates the visible search widget in the complementary region and scrapes results. Per the KB guidance this helper is the correct first attempt. The previous run showed the helper failed because the complementary region was not found; therefore I call it within a try/except so that a failure falls back to a manual, accessibility-role-centric approach.\n\n2) If the helper succeeds, I locate role=\"main\" and its role=\"article\" children, count them with await locator.count(), compute the third-to-last index (count - 3), and click the first link inside that article (falling back to clicking the article itself if no links exist). I ensure to call await locator.count() before .nth() to avoid indexing errors.\n\n3) If the helper fails or does not produce clickable results, I search for a complementary region (role=\"complementary\") and within it a role=\"searchbox\" or role=\"textbox\". If none exists, I search for global role=\"searchbox\" or role=\"textbox\". These are the KB helper's own fallbacks but implemented manually here because the KB helper couldn't find the complementary region in the prior run.\n\n4) Once a search input is located, I fill it with 'crypto' and attempt to activate the search by clicking a button inside the same parent region named with /search/i (case-insensitive). If that fails I click the first button in the same region. If there are no buttons, I press Enter in the input as the last accessibility-role-centric fallback.\n\n5) After triggering the search I wait for role=\"main\" to appear (or fall back to networkidle) then again locate articles and click the third-to-last item as described above. Every locator-based index uses await count() before .nth().\n\n6) I included robust error handling to raise clear exceptions when a search widget cannot be found or when there are not enough search results to perform the required click.\n\nUtility of KB functions:\n- create_curated_collection_by_search: Highly relevant and the correct helper for the visible-search action; attempted first. Because it previously failed (no complementary region) I catch exceptions and fall back to manual role-based interactions.\n- create_curated_collection_and_newsletter: Not relevant to this narrow task, so not used.\n\nBecause the task is not yet completed in the previous run, this act() implements new actions (attempting the helper again and then manual search activation if needed) to reach the goal of searching for 'crypto' and clicking the third-to-last result.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    \"\"\"\n    Attempt plan (step-by-step):\n    1. Try the site-aware helper create_curated_collection_by_search first (it is the KB helper designed for using the visible search UI).\n       - If it succeeds and renders results, locate the role=\"main\" role=\"article\" children and click the third-to-last result.\n    2. If the helper fails (previous run indicated the complementary region is missing), fall back to a manual accessibility-role-centric approach:\n       - Look for a complementary region and inside it a role=\"searchbox\" or role=\"textbox\". If found, fill it with 'crypto' and activate the search via a button named with /search/i or the first button in that region. If no button, press Enter in the input.\n       - If no complementary region is present, look for a global role=\"searchbox\" or role=\"textbox\" and use it similarly.\n       - After triggering the search, wait for role=\"main\" to appear (or use networkidle as fallback) and then click the third-to-last article's first link (or the article itself as a fallback).\n\n    Notes about knowledge-base function usage:\n    - create_curated_collection_by_search is the appropriate helper to perform a visible search. Per instructions and the knowledge-base guidance, we attempt to use it first. Because it itself navigates to '/', it may fail if the site lacks the expected complementary region; we therefore catch exceptions and fall back to manual actions.\n    \"\"\"\n    import re\n\n    async def click_third_to_last_from_main():\n        main = page.get_by_role(\"main\")\n        articles = main.get_by_role(\"article\")\n        cnt = await articles.count()\n        if cnt < 3:\n            return False\n        target_idx = cnt - 3\n        target = articles.nth(target_idx)\n        # prefer first link inside the article\n        link_locator = target.get_by_role(\"link\")\n        link_count = await link_locator.count()\n        if link_count > 0:\n            await link_locator.nth(0).click()\n        else:\n            await target.click()\n        return True\n\n    # 1) Try the KB helper first\n    try:\n        posts = await create_curated_collection_by_search(page, \"crypto\", max_results=0, resolve_links=True)\n    except Exception:\n        posts = None\n\n    # If helper produced something, attempt to click third-to-last from the rendered main region\n    try:\n        if posts is not None:\n            # Give the page a brief moment if rendering is still happening\n            try:\n                await page.get_by_role(\"main\").wait_for(state=\"visible\", timeout=3000)\n            except Exception:\n                # ignore and proceed to try locating articles\n                pass\n\n            clicked = await click_third_to_last_from_main()\n            if clicked:\n                return\n    except Exception:\n        # Fall through to manual approach\n        pass\n\n    # 2) Manual fallback: search inside complementary region first (accessibility-role-centric)\n    try:\n        comp_count = await page.get_by_role(\"complementary\").count()\n    except Exception:\n        comp_count = 0\n\n    search_input = None\n    search_parent = None\n\n    if comp_count > 0:\n        comp = page.get_by_role(\"complementary\").nth(0)\n        # prefer role=searchbox inside complementary\n        if await comp.get_by_role(\"searchbox\").count() > 0:\n            search_input = comp.get_by_role(\"searchbox\").nth(0)\n            search_parent = comp\n        elif await comp.get_by_role(\"textbox\").count() > 0:\n            search_input = comp.get_by_role(\"textbox\").nth(0)\n            search_parent = comp\n\n    # If no complementary, try global search controls\n    if search_input is None:\n        if await page.get_by_role(\"searchbox\").count() > 0:\n            search_input = page.get_by_role(\"searchbox\").nth(0)\n            search_parent = page\n        elif await page.get_by_role(\"textbox\").count() > 0:\n            search_input = page.get_by_role(\"textbox\").nth(0)\n            search_parent = page\n\n    if search_input is None:\n        # Unable to find a search widget via accessibility roles; give up explicitly\n        raise Exception(\"Search widget (role=searchbox or role=textbox) not found on the page; cannot perform the requested search.\")\n\n    # Fill the search input\n    await search_input.fill(\"crypto\")\n\n    # Try to find a button with name containing 'search' inside the same parent region (accessibility-centric)\n    clicked_search_trigger = False\n    if search_parent is not None:\n        try:\n            # use regex name match for 'search' (case-insensitive)\n            btn_locator = search_parent.get_by_role(\"button\", name=re.compile(r\"search\", re.I))\n            if await btn_locator.count() > 0:\n                await btn_locator.nth(0).click()\n                clicked_search_trigger = True\n        except Exception:\n            # ignore and try other fallbacks\n            clicked_search_trigger = False\n\n        if not clicked_search_trigger:\n            # fallback: click first button inside the parent region\n            try:\n                first_btn = search_parent.get_by_role(\"button\")\n                if await first_btn.count() > 0:\n                    await first_btn.nth(0).click()\n                    clicked_search_trigger = True\n            except Exception:\n                clicked_search_trigger = False\n\n    # If no button triggered, press Enter in the input as last resort\n    if not clicked_search_trigger:\n        await search_input.press(\"Enter\")\n\n    # Wait for results: prefer role=main appearing\n    try:\n        await page.get_by_role(\"main\").wait_for(state=\"visible\", timeout=5000)\n    except Exception:\n        # fallback to waiting for network idle\n        try:\n            await page.wait_for_load_state(\"networkidle\")\n        except Exception:\n            # give a short grace period and continue\n            import asyncio\n\n            await asyncio.sleep(1)\n\n    # Finally, click the third-to-last article in main\n    success = await click_third_to_last_from_main()\n    if not